    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d233">

                <pb facs="0264" n="148" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg148"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 893.51/1014.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the
                            Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-07-24">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 24, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Chinese Government has asked the Baldwin Locomotive Works for a loan of
                    £300,000 for five or eight years on condition that during that period China
                    purchase locomotives to the same amount. The Baldwin agent here wishes to know
                    the attitude of the American Government. I call attention to the negotiations
                    proceeding with various commercial houses, probably without the consent of their
                    Governments, which seem likely to secure for Europe for some years to come
                    Chinese markets in which the United States is interested.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Calhoun.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
